    "q-47596": {
      "dom_id": "step-q47596",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14202292/assets/d9e89a9b811bdc6b766b01eecf817df48968e3ea604b89bc55bc9660331194e0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=4$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=1$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Both limits ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ do not exist because the function $f(x)$ is not given in the interval $(0,2].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
