    "q-67539": {
      "dom_id": "step-q67539",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14202292/assets/1e40e0ffa7880b3b3b63070f063de207dcfafbdbed5fdf02464e180feba715e2.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},3).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=3$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nWhen we approach $x=3$ from the left, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-67539-a",
